Third, \textsc{oracle} is the most \emph{spatially consistent} policy we
measured. Its per-cell hit map is the most concentrated of any arm (Gini
$0.400$ versus $0.316$--$0.338$ for the other rectangle arms): it repeatedly
asks the encoder to represent the same region of the image. This suggests a
mechanism other than anatomical correctness --- consistency of the predictive
task --- which we return to in Section~\ref{sec:limits}.

\subsection{Fine-tuning narrows but does not erase the gap}

Under full fine-tuning rather than a frozen probe, \textsc{oracle} reaches
$0.8947$ (mean-pool head) against \textsc{random}'s $0.8868$, a gap of
$+0.0079$ compared with $+0.0109$ frozen. The pretraining difference is
partially, but not entirely, absorbed by supervised adaptation --- consistent
with the masking policy shaping the representation rather than merely the
optimisation start point.

\subsection{Subgroup analysis}
\label{sec:subgroup}

Joining saved test predictions to demographics in deterministic loader order
(validated by exact label reconstruction and in-volume attribute checks), Black
participants have the lowest AUC in every valid probe. The disparity persists
across masking policies and across epochs, which indicates it is inherited from
the data and downstream task rather than introduced by any particular
pretraining objective. It is not removed by the policy that maximises aggregate
AUC. No individual arm's gap is significant at these subgroup sizes
($n{=}431$ Black, $n{=}251$ Asian against $n{=}2318$ white); the defensible
claim is the consistency of the ordering across 12 independently fitted probes,
not the magnitude of any one gap. Appendix~\ref{app:subgroup} gives the numbers.

A second stratification is more robust than the racial one and is, in our view,
the more consequential clinical result. Scoring each disease-severity stratum
against the shared pool of all negatives, every arm is far weaker on mild
disease (AUC $0.791$--$0.823$) than on severe disease ($0.928$--$0.956$). The
severe-to-mild gap is $0.131$--$0.139$ in \emph{all} 12 probes --- a spread of
$0.009$ across policies whose aggregate AUCs differ by $0.032$ and whose racial
gaps differ twofold. Mild and moderate disease account for 1{,}132 of the 1{,}466
positives and are the clinically actionable window. No target-placement policy
tested here moves it. We regard this as the strongest evidence in the study that
masking-policy design, in either direction, is not the lever that matters for
this task. We report both analyses as secondary findings and caution that no
policy studied here should be presented as a fairness intervention.

\subsection{Reproducibility}

The epoch-100 \textsc{oracle} encoder and its trained probe head are released.
Re-encoding the test split on different hardware, at fp32 rather than fp16, and
with a different encoder chunk size reproduces the reported AUC to
$9.8\times10^{-6}$ ($0.8854754$ versus $0.8854852$) --- 22 discordant pairs out
of $2{,}248{,}844$. Probe-time numerical precision is therefore immaterial to
every comparison reported here, a check we recommend for any study reporting AUC
differences of order $10^{-2}$.

\section{Discussion and limitations}
\label{sec:limits}

\paragraph{What the evidence supports.}
Guidance helps: restricting targets to tissue is worth $+0.0120$ at the matched
epoch, and a consistent intensity-located band is worth $+0.0109$ at epoch 100,
with a paired confidence interval excluding zero. A trained segmentation model is
not required to obtain this benefit, and using one more aggressively --- to shape
targets or to maximise anatomy coverage --- does not add to it. For practitioners
the implication is concrete: a segmentation stage can be removed from this
pipeline without measured loss.

\paragraph{What it does not support.}
We cannot claim anatomy-shaped masking is \emph{harmful}: at the matched epoch it
sits $+0.0013$ above the null, statistically indistinguishable rather than worse,
and we have no confidence interval for that contrast. Nor can we claim target
\emph{shape} is the operative variable, for the reasons in
Table~\ref{tab:geom}. Nor can we claim the mechanism behind \textsc{oracle}'s
advantage. Two explanations remain live: consistency of location (the encoder is
repeatedly forced to represent the same region), and masking ratio or task
difficulty. Distinguishing them requires an arm that randomises the band's
vertical position while holding area and context fixed, which we did not run.

\paragraph{Only one contrast carries an interval.}
The paired bootstrap was computed for \textsc{oracle} versus \textsc{random} at
epoch 100. The other contrasts we report --- including the $+0.0120$ for
\textsc{envelope} --- are point differences on the same fixed test set without
interval estimates, and should be read accordingly.

\paragraph{One pretraining run per policy.}
The paired bootstrap quantifies sampling error on a fixed test set; it does not
quantify seed-to-seed variance of pretraining. With $n{=}1$ continuation per arm
the \emph{ranking} is not statistically established even where individual
pairwise differences are significant on this test set. This is the principal
limitation of the study, and it applies to every arm comparison we report.

\paragraph{One visible-tissue floor.}
Only $f{=}0.21$ was pretrained for \textsc{cover}, so we report no dose-response
over the floor and make no claim that any floor value is optimal.

\paragraph{Single dataset, single task.}
All results are glaucoma classification from FairVision OCT. Generality to other
modalities, diseases, or segmentation-quality regimes is untested. In particular,
the anatomy arms depend on the quality of MIRAGE's predictions on this data; a
better segmenter might change the conclusion.

\paragraph{One arm is still training.}
The \textsc{cover} $f{=}0.21$ arm has probes at epochs 27, 30, 34 and 50; its
epoch-75 and epoch-100 probes had not completed at submission time and are marked
as pending rather than reported. Its epoch-50 value is included because it is a
matched-epoch comparison against every other arm. We draw no conclusion about
that policy's endpoint behaviour.

\paragraph{Broader impact and ethics.}
This study uses a public, de-identified retinal dataset under its release terms
and involves no new data collection or human subjects. No model reported here is
clinically validated or intended for deployment; the frozen-probe AUCs are
research measurements on one dataset, not evidence of clinical utility. The
subgroup finding in Section~\ref{sec:subgroup} is a caution rather than a
contribution: the best-performing policy does not close the gap, and we would
regard deployment of any of these encoders without a dedicated fairness
evaluation as inappropriate. Releasing pretrained weights carries the usual dual
-use consideration that they may be applied to populations unlike the training
cohort; we document the cohort composition so that mismatch is detectable.

\section{Conclusion}

We tested the intuition that masked predictive pretraining in medical imaging
should target clinically meaningful anatomy. Under matched compute, schedule, and
a shared ancestor checkpoint, guidance helped --- but anatomical precision did
not. Restricting targets to retinal tissue improved AUC over unguided masking by
$+0.0120$ at the matched epoch, while shaping targets to trace the segmented
retina, or maximising anatomy coverage, left performance indistinguishable from
not guiding at all; and the best policy in the study consulted no segmentation
model. Direct measurement of mask geometry shows the ordering is uncorrelated
with how much anatomy is hidden. We suggest the operative variable may be the
consistency of the predictive task rather than its anatomical correctness, and we
release weights and the reproduction protocol so the claim can be checked.

