\section{Subgroup and severity analysis}
\label{app:subgroup}

All numbers below come from saved test predictions joined to FairVision's
self-reported attributes in deterministic loader order; the join is validated by
exact label reconstruction. Twelve non-retracted frozen probes are used. These
span only five pretraining runs (envelope, anatomy-v2, cover, anatomy-v1, and
the shared ancestor), so probes within a family share an encoder and a seed:
the effective independent $n$ is 5, not 12. The four retracted coverage probes
of Appendix~\ref{app:excluded} are excluded here as everywhere.

\paragraph{Race.} Black participants have the lowest AUC in 12 of 12
non-retracted probes. Table~\ref{tab:race} shows a representative arm with
2000-sample bootstrap confidence intervals. The Black interval overlaps the
white interval in every arm, so no single gap is individually significant; only
the consistency of the ordering is.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Race-stratified AUC, \textsc{anatomy-v2} @ep50, 2000-sample bootstrap.}
\label{tab:race}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lrrcc}
\toprule
group & $n$ & $n_{+}$ & AUC & 95\% CI \\
\midrule
Asian & 251 & 118 & 0.9058 & $[0.8708, 0.9424]$ \\
white & 2318 & 1080 & 0.8672 & $[0.8515, 0.8809]$ \\
Black & 431 & 268 & \textbf{0.8123} & $[0.7711, 0.8507]$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The size of the racial gap varies about twofold across policies
($0.0475$ for \textsc{cover} @ep34 to $0.0935$ for \textsc{anatomy-v2} @ep50)
while aggregate AUC varies by only $0.032$. We do not interpret this ordering
causally: policy family is perfectly confounded with pretraining seed, and
epochs are not matched across families. Spearman correlation between aggregate
AUC and racial gap over the 12 probes is $\rho{=}0.427$, $p{=}0.167$; we
therefore explicitly do \emph{not} claim that more accurate models are less fair.

\paragraph{Disease severity.} FairVision's label is defined by visual-field
mean deviation (md): every test volume with md $\leq -2$ is positive. Severity
therefore cannot be used as an ordinary subgroup axis, because within-bin AUC is
undefined --- a trap that silently yields nothing under naive stratification.
Instead each positive stratum is scored against the shared pool of all 1{,}534
negatives (Table~\ref{tab:severity}).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Severity-stratified AUC. Each positive stratum is scored against the
shared pool of all 1{,}534 negatives. Ranges span all 12 valid probes.}
\label{tab:severity}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lrc}
\toprule
stratum & $n_{+}$ & AUC range across 12 arms \\
\midrule
severe (md $\leq -12$) & 334 & 0.928 -- 0.956 \\
moderate ($-12 <$ md $\leq -6$) & 460 & 0.870 -- 0.910 \\
mild ($-6 <$ md $\leq -2$) & 672 & 0.791 -- 0.823 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The severe-to-mild gap is $0.1306$--$0.1394$ in every one of the 12 arms. That
spread of $0.009$ is smaller than the $0.032$ spread in aggregate AUC and far
smaller than the twofold variation in racial gap, which is why we regard it as
the most policy-invariant effect in the study.

\paragraph{Limitations.} One dataset, one test split, reused throughout the
programme's history. Race and ethnicity are self-reported categories inherited
from FairVision's coding; at these subgroup sizes we cannot speak to
intersectional groups. All figures are frozen mean-pool linear probes;
disparities could differ under fine-tuning or a stronger head.

\section{Excluded runs}
\label{app:excluded}

Four probes of an earlier coverage arm are excluded from all analysis. They were
trained with half-precision EMA targets and a different encoder-truncation
policy from the arms they would be compared against, so their deficit is not
attributable to masking. We report their existence for completeness and do not
use them to support any claim. Similarly, the \textsc{anatomy-v2} probes at
epochs 75 and 92 follow a change of target precision partway through that arm's
training; they are listed in Table~\ref{tab:allprobes} but excluded from the
matched-epoch comparisons in Table~\ref{tab:main}.

